  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Thermal-plex shows early but concrete reach for a 2023 open-access methods paper: the record shows 30 downloads, 239 views, and one documented downstream reuse in a biomedical article mention. The broader citation and discussion trail is still sparse, so the strongest story here is that the work is accessible and beginning to be reused rather than widely cited.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Thermal-plex, authored by a Harvard-centered team with contributors from Harvard University and Howard Hughes Medical Institute, presents a fluidic-free approach to rapid sequential multiplexed imaging using DNA-encoded thermal channels. The paper sits squarely in multiplexed fluorescence imaging, microfluidics, and molecular imaging, and its contribution is methodological: it addresses a practical bottleneck in sequential imaging by removing the need for slow buffer exchanges.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">In terms of measured uptake, the clearest signals are usage and reuse. Zenodo records 239 views and 30 downloads, indicating that the work has been found and accessed, while the reuse graph shows one documented downstream reuse in a biomedical article mention. That reuse is meaningful because it shows the work has already entered a subsequent scientific record, even though the citation count in OpenAlex is still 0.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding attention picture is modest. There are no scite citation classifications or event-data mentions recorded, and public-discourse archiving is unavailable in this digest, so the evidence base for broader conversation is limited. This makes the article\u2019s impact story an early-stage one: visible through repository access and a documented mention, but not yet through accumulated citations or a large public footprint.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Stewardship signals support the work\u2019s accessibility and persistence. The record identifies Zenodo as the hosting repository, with an open instance available, and the article is described as open access. Taken together, the available signals suggest a methods paper that is already usable by others and positioned for continued dissemination, even as formal citation-based impact remains minimal at this stage.</p>",
